<article class="page-shell section"><a class="text-link mb-8" href={back}><ArrowLeft aria-hidden="true" size={16} strokeWidth={1.5} /> All notes</a><h1 class="section-heading">A home for experiments.</h1><p class="my-6 text-sm text-muted-foreground">September 26, 2026 · Build notes</p><div class="prose-copy"><p>A personal website can do more than introduce its owner. It can be a place to try an idea, keep a useful tool within reach, and make the work visible while it is still evolving.</p><h2>Start with something useful</h2><p>This workspace starts with three calculators: Stripe fees, auto loans, and mortgages. Each has a small job. Inputs remain editable, assumptions remain visible, and the calculations run in the browser without an account.</p><p>The tools can work backwards, too. A desired remaining payment becomes a required charge. A monthly loan budget becomes a possible purchase price. Starting from the outcome often makes a decision easier to explore.</p><h2>Share the foundations</h2><p>The workspace keeps typography, color, spacing, and reusable controls in one UI package. A live design-system page puts those choices beside the components that use them. A new experiment can look like it belongs here without copying another page’s CSS.</p><h2>Leave room for different approaches</h2><p>The portfolio uses Astro for pages and React for interactive controls. The sculpture is a Three.js torus knot with a short GSAP introduction. These are independent decisions: a future app can use a different framework without replacing the portfolio.</p><p>A pnpm workspace provides those boundaries. Each app can be deployed to its own subdomain, while shared packages contain only the pieces that are actually shared.</p><h2>Keep it open-ended</h2><p>This is a starting point for tools, learning notes, and experiments. The useful question for the next addition is simple: what would be worth coming back to?</p></div></article>
